<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Co-occurring alcohol use disorder and post-traumatic stress disorder in a 44-year-old veteran. Please confirm the PTSD diagnosis, provide trauma-focused psychotherapy alongside his alcohol treatment, and share your assessment of risk. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does he meet criteria for PTSD, and is a PCL-5 of 39 a reasonable baseline for measuring progress?</item><item>Which trauma-focused psychotherapy do you recommend, and can it start while he is still drinking?</item><item>How should his alcohol treatment be coordinated with trauma therapy, and is there a point at which you would advise adding medication?</item><item>Please reassess suicide risk and help keep his safety plan current.</item><item>What do you advise for his sleep and nightmares?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Boyington was referred by Dr. Marquez for evaluation and trauma-focused psychotherapy. At the first session in early July a structured diagnostic interview confirmed post-traumatic stress disorder related to combat and captivity, with intrusive memories, nightmares, avoidance of reminders, hypervigilance, and startle. He chose prolonged exposure therapy and has attended five weekly sessions. The run of nightmares that followed the start of imaginal exposure in the third week was followed by one day of heavy drinking; he used his relapse plan and returned to the next session. He now drinks on about four days in six weeks, has fewer nightmares, and startles less. He reports no suicidal thoughts since intake and keeps his safety plan with him. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Gregory Boyington was born December 4, 1912, in Coeur d’Alene, Idaho. [D]</item><item>Boyington commanded Marine Fighting Squadron 214 and received the Medal of Honor. [D]</item><item>Boyington was shot down January 3, 1944, and spent approximately 20 months as a Japanese prisoner of war. [D]</item><item>Marine Corps reporting describes Boyington as beaten, interrogated, and nearly starved during captivity. [D]</item><item>A military scholarly review of a researched biography documents longstanding alcohol-related impairment, periods of sobriety, and later relapses. [D]</item><item>Post-traumatic stress disorder is a retrospective interpretation of Boyington’s combat and captivity history, not a documented lifetime diagnosis. [R]</item><item>The roster’s phrase ‘eventual sobriety’ overstates a recurrent course that included both sustained sober periods and relapse. [R]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

