<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Post-traumatic stress disorder (F43.10). Diagnosis confirmed. The intake PCL-5 of 39 is a valid baseline. Today&apos;s 31 is an 8-point fall, short of the 10 points usually taken as meaningful change, but the course is early and nightmares and startle are improving. [S]</item><item>Alcohol use disorder (F10.20). Drinking has fallen from most days to about four days in six weeks (AUDIT-C 10 to 5). The single heavy-drinking day followed the expected rise in distress early in exposure work, not disengagement. Concurrent treatment of both disorders is appropriate and does not need to wait for abstinence. [S]</item><item>Suicide risk. Low acute risk: no current ideation, intent, plan, or preparatory behavior, and PHQ-9 item 9 is 0. Chronic risk factors are male sex, veteran status, alcohol use disorder, and PTSD; protective factors are engagement in treatment, veteran and recovery peers, family support, and a strong sense of service. The safety plan was reviewed with him today and remains current. [S]</item><item>Sleep disturbance with nightmares. Nightmares are part of the PTSD and are already easing with exposure therapy and less drinking. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Marquez&apos;s referral and intake safety assessment, and today&apos;s AUDIT-C, PCL-5, and PHQ-9 alongside the intake scores. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

